content.thinking:
The "Saving an edit on blur" scenario fails. The blur handler defers the save with setTimeout(0), so the assertion runs before the save happens.

Let me look at the step definition for "the user types {string} in the edit input and blurs":

```javascript
When('the user types {string} in the edit input and blurs', function (text) {
  typeInEditInput(this.document, text);
  blurEditInput(this.document);
});
```

And `blurEditInput`:
```javascript
function blurEditInput(doc) {
  const input = getEditInput(doc);
  if (input) {
    input.dispatchEvent(new doc.defaultView.Event('blur', { bubbles: true }));
  }
}
```

The blur event is dispatched synchronously. My handler runs synchronously and schedules a setTimeout(0). The assertion runs immediately after, before the setTimeout fires.

I need to make the blur save synchronous for normal blur. But I also need to handle Escape-initiated blur.

The issue is that I can't distinguish between normal blur and Escape-initiated blur at blur time in the harness. The harness always fires blur before keydown for Escape.

One solution: make the blur handler synchronous, and have the Escape keydown handler undo the save if Escape was pressed.

But the Escape keydown fires AFTER blur in the harness. So:
1. Blur fires → save "Buy milk" (synchronous)
2. Keydown fires → restore "Buy groceries" (synchronous)
3. Assertion runs → todo has "Buy groceries"

This should work! The net effect is correct. Let me try making the blur handler synchronous.

Actually wait, the issue is that the Escape keydown handler needs to know the original value to restore it. Let me use the `originalEditValue` variable I added.


When the user double-clicks, I set `originalEditValue` to the current title. The blur handler saves the new value, and then the Escape keydown handler restores the original value. This way the blur save happens immediately, but Escape can still undo it.

